title: Selectors
tags: [[Command line]]
kind: reference
summary: -i one metarecord, -q a query (-s for simplified text), nothing for all — an option before the verb, so every verb can use it.

`mf metarecord` picks its target with a ''selector option placed before the
verb'', so one selector serves every verb, `field` included:

|!Option |!Selects |
|`-i <uuid>` |one metarecord, by uuid (no query) |
|`-q "<query>"` |the metarecords matching a query ([[Writing queries]]) |
|`-s` |with `-q`: the text is the simplified language, expanded first — in the client, never by the daemon |
|(none) |every metarecord |

`-q` and `-i` are mutually exclusive; `-s` needs `-q`. `mf tag` adds `--eq
name[:type]=value` (repeatable, AND-ed: an exact match).

''The option decides, never the shape of its text.'' A bare 32-hex uuid is a valid
query, so `-q <uuid>` is an ordinary query and behaves like every other `-q`, while
`-i <uuid>` keeps the single-resource behaviour. The two differ only in the output
of `get` (full JSON for `-i`, uuids for `-q`) and in what `field get --resolve`
accepts; every other verb sends both as the same set request. Where a target is a
''positional'' argument instead (`mf path`, the targets of `mf tag`), no option was
typed, and the shape of the text decides.

```
mf -n music metarecord -q 'mfr_path ->* "/docs"' field set reviewed:bool=true
```
